research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-cex-mean-value-equality-fails-for-a-vector-valued-function.md-407-## Remarks
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-cex-mean-value-equality-fails-for-a-vector-valued-function.md-408-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-cex-mean-value-equality-fails-for-a-vector-valued-function.md-409-- **What exactly fails.** The scalar mean value theorem is applied in the proof of [[thm-mean-value-inequality]] to the auxiliary function $t \mapsto \langle u, f(t)\rangle$ for the fixed vector $u = f(b)-f(a)$, and it does produce a point $\xi$. That $\xi$ is a mean value point of **that real function**, and it depends on $u$; there is no reason for the two coordinates to be served by one and the same point, and on this curve they are not: the first coordinate demands $\xi = 1/\iota(2)$ and the second demands $\iota(3)\xi^{2} = 1$.
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-cex-mean-value-equality-fails-for-a-vector-valued-function.md-410-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-cex-mean-value-equality-fails-for-a-vector-valued-function.md-411-- **The curve is not pathological.** Both components are polynomials, so $f$ is differentiable at every real ([[lem-derivative-of-a-power]]) and $f'$ is continuous. No smoothness hypothesis would rescue the equality form.
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-cex-mean-value-equality-fails-for-a-vector-valued-function.md-412-
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-cex-mean-value-equality-fails-for-a-vector-valued-function.md:413:- **The bound of [[thm-mean-value-inequality]] is not merely true but sharp**, and the companion item [[cex-the-mean-value-inequality-is-attained]] exhibits a curve on which it is an equality. The two items together show that the failure of the equality **form** is not a failure of the bound.
research/audit/wave12-certifier-tasks/a6-cex-mean-value-equality-fails-for-a-vector-valued-function.md-414-````
